{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>Benjamin is a wolf that tears prey.</b> It alludes to Saul, who acted in the manner of a wolf that tears swiftly, for the habit of the wolf is that it does not delay in its prey, but tears at great speed. So too did he act, and he did not wait for the time that Samuel had fixed for him, but hurried (I Samuel 13:8) to tear — and from this came the cause that he ate only in the morning, and his kingdom was cut short." "And its saying <b>and at evening</b> means: at the evening time of his kingdom he will divide, when they would say (I Samuel 18:7) ״Saul has smitten, etc., and David in his tens of thousands.״ Or it may hint that through the intermediary of his cause the kingdom of the house of David will be divided, along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Yoma 22b): when David said ״You and Ziba shall divide the field,״ a bat kol went forth and said: ״Rehoboam and Jeroboam shall divide the kingdom״ — and ever since the sun of Israel set, because of our sins."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:50:56.726276838Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}